    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "The letters B, A, L, L, O, O, N, S are written on cards and put in a hat. One card is selected at random and its letter is recorded.\na. List the letter on each card, including repeats.\nb. List the sample space.\nc. How many different outcomes are in the sample space?",
        "solution_text": "a. B, A, L, L, O, O, N, S\nb. Write each different letter once.\nSample space $= \\{B, A, L, O, N, S\\}$\nc. There are $6$ different outcomes."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Some number cards are put in a box. The sample space for drawing one card and recording its number is $\\{4, 7, 9\\}$.\na. Is $\\{9, 7, 4\\}$ the same sample space?\nb. Could a card show $5$?\nc. Can you tell how many cards show odd numbers?\nd. Does the sample space tell you that each number is equally likely?",
        "solution_text": "a. Yes. Both lists contain the same numbers, each written once. The order does not matter.\nb. No. $5$ is not in the sample space.\nc. No. The odd numbers are $7$ and $9$, but the sample space does not show how many cards carry each number.\nd. No. The sample space shows which numbers are possible, not how many cards carry each number."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "The spinner has six equal sectors. It is spun and the colour of the sector is recorded. G means green, P means purple and R means red.\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont}]\n\\draw (0,0) circle (1.6);\n\\foreach \\a in {30,90,150,210,270,330} {\\draw (0,0)--(\\a:1.6);}\n\\node at (60:1.05) {G};\n\\node at (0:1.05) {G};\n\\node at (300:1.05) {G};\n\\node at (240:1.05) {P};\n\\node at (180:1.05) {P};\n\\node at (120:1.05) {R};\n\\draw[->,thick] (0,0)--(35:0.85);\n\\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]\na. List the colour label on each sector, including repeats.\nb. List the sample space.\nc. Mia says the sample space is $\\{G,G,G,P,P,R\\}$. Is she correct?\nd. How many different outcomes are in the sample space?\ne. Is each colour equally likely?",
        "solution_text": "a. $G,G,G,P,P,R$\nb. Write each different colour once: $S=\\{G,P,R\\}$.\nc. No. Mia has listed the six sector labels. A sample space contains no repeated outcomes.\nd. There are $3$ different outcomes, although there are $6$ sectors.\ne. No. Green covers $3$ equal sectors, purple covers $2$ and red covers $1$. The colours are not equally likely."
      }
    ],
